  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1205 PM MDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nThe upper level ridge will be the main player in our weather over\nthe next week. The upper level ridge will control how much\nmoisture and where the moisture makes an impact as it meanders\naround the Southwest. Currently the ridge is centered over \nArizona and the circulation around the ridge continues to pull in \nsome drier air aloft. Like yesterday, this will again limit our \nrain chances. And again like yesterday, the best chances for \nthunderstorms will be limited to the Gila Region. The main threat\nfrom the thunderstorms will be strong outflow winds. High \ntemperatures for today will jump back near or above the 100\ndegrees across the lowlands.\n\nTuesday will be similar with the upper level ridge centered along\nthe New Mexico/Arizona border and that slightly closer proximity\nwill allow our high temperatures to creep up a few more degrees\nwhich will allow El Paso the Rio Grande Valley southeast of town\nto hit heat advisory criteria (105F). Wednesday looks similar so\nthe heat advisory may need to be extended into Wednesday\nafternoon. Also both Tuesday and Wednesday we will continue to see\na chance for thunderstorms in the Gila Region, but the rest of the\narea looks to stay dry. \n\nFor Thursday and Friday, the upper level ridge will drift a little\nnorth to near the Arizona/Utah border. This will allow the\nthunderstorm threat to spread to the Sacramento mountains and the\nlowlands west of the Rio Grande on Thursday and we will see at\nleast a slight chance for thunderstorms across the whole area on\nFriday. High temperatures both Thursday and Friday will drop a\ncouple of degrees, but still run at or above 100F in the lowlands.\nFor the weekend, the upper level ridge shifts closer to the Four\nCorners region which will again limit our thunderstorm coverage to\narea mountains and continue our high temperatures running near or\na little above the 100F mark.",
